# AGENTS.md and its CLAUDE.md symlink are tracked on purpose: orienting a fresh agent is
# the point of them. Only the Windows download artefact stays ignored.
*:Zone.Identifier
*.pyc
__pycache__/
*.png
artifacts/*
qwen3-06b-grpo-math-quick/*

# uv creates .venv from uv.lock; the lockfile is tracked, the tree is not. The bare name rather
# than `.venv/` on purpose: a directory-only pattern does not match the symlink a composed gate
# worktree carries, and an untracked symlink walks the privacy sweep into ~97,000 venv files.
.venv
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# Agent and editor state, none of which is this repo's content. Tracked here rather than left to
# `.git/info/exclude` and a global ignore in the owner's home directory, because neither of those
# travels with a clone and both currently name individual files under `.claude/` rather than the
# directory, so a new one appears untracked and stageable. Two things ride on it: three permission
# entries in `.claude/settings.local.json` carry an AWS profile name, and agent worktrees live under
# `.claude/worktrees/`, which `git ls-files --others` emits as a directory that
# `scripts/scan_secrets.py` then walks -- tens of GB with a `.venv` in each, hard-erroring on the
# first file over the sweep's 64 MiB limit. Bare names for the same reason as `.venv` above: a
# directory-only pattern would not match a symlink of the same name. `[tool.basedpyright] exclude`
# in pyproject.toml lists `.claude` for the worktree half of this.
.claude
.cursor
.idea
.vscode
.direnv
.DS_Store

# Scratch notes stay local. This repo is public and working notes are not written with an
# audience in mind. The README stays tracked so a fresh clone still explains what the
# otherwise-empty directory is for. Consequence to know about: code comments and docs
# throughout the repo point at files in here, and those pointers dangle in a fresh clone, so
# anything that becomes load-bearing belongs in docs/ proper rather than here.
# Ignore the contents rather than the directory itself: git will not descend into an excluded
# directory, so `docs/scratch/` here would make the README negation below unreachable.
docs/scratch/*
!docs/scratch/README.md

# Plans, handoffs, research-direction notes and the directions index are internal working
# material: they name people, quote private decisions, and describe benchmark items. The remote
# is public, so they live in `docs/scratch/` only — the single scratch location. These entries
# make re-tracking them an explicit act rather than an accident, since a fresh agent's instinct
# is to write a plan doc under `docs/plans/`. The `notes/` directory was folded into
# `docs/scratch/` (2026-08-18); its entry stays as a tripwire against re-accretion.
docs/plans/
docs/research-directions/
docs/future.md
notes/

# The baked case files the harness task registry reads at import. Local-only on purpose, and not
# because they are large: the Impossible-LiveCodeBench derivation carries problem statements and
# test bodies with no redistribution grant anywhere in its provenance chain, and this remote is
# public, so republishing them would be both a licence problem and a contamination one. The
# EvalPlus file derives from public saturated benchmarks and is untracked for the contamination
# half of that reason alone. Both are regenerated by the in-repo ETLs; the directory keeps a
# tracked README with the commands, which is why the contents are ignored rather than the
# directory (git will not descend into an excluded directory, so the negation below needs the
# glob form).
reward_hacking/harness/data/*
!reward_hacking/harness/data/README.md

# Survey instrument item text, read by the eval battery's self-report section. Local-only on
# purpose, and licensing before size: the stems, anchors and payoff tables were transcribed from
# copyrighted papers, exactly one of the seven instruments carries a redistribution grant, and this
# remote is public. Contamination is the second reason -- an instrument published in a training
# corpus stops measuring the before-and-after delta the section exists for. The loader, schema, item
# ids, subscale keys, reverse-keying and every scoring formula are tracked in games/survey.py; only
# the strings are here, and games/data/survey/README.md carries the schema.
#
# Four lines rather than two because the file sits one level down. Git will not descend into an
# excluded directory, so `!games/data/survey/README.md` under a bare `games/data/*` would be
# unreachable and the README would be silently untracked -- the exact failure the two stanzas above
# warn about, one directory deeper. So: exclude the data root's contents, re-include the survey
# directory so git descends into it, exclude that directory's contents, re-include its README.
# `tests/test_doc_links.py` and `git check-ignore -v games/data/survey/README.md` are how to tell
# this still holds after an edit.
games/data/*
!games/data/survey/
games/data/survey/*
!games/data/survey/README.md

# The canary baseline is a list of absolute paths on one machine, keyed to that machine's home
# directory, and every hash in it is meaningless anywhere else. It is written by
# `scripts/canary_manifest.py --update` and stays local; the README explains the convention.
canary/*
!canary/README.md

# Per-run launch kits carry the bucket, region and profile literals a box needs; the tracked
# reference they are copied from is scripts/kit_reference/. Ignored files do not ship in the code
# archive (scripts/ship_tree.sh lists with --exclude-standard), so a kit's runner and checks travel
# to the box as sha256-pinned --data objects; see the reference kit's README, customisation step 2.
scripts/kits/

# Hand-carried model/record exports between machines; they hold benchmark item text, never commit.
/model-export-*

# Backlog.md config (local task tool; its tasks live under docs/scratch/backlog)
backlog.config.yml
